The SHG tensor vanishes identically in any centrosymmetric crystal, so only acentric structures can contribute. Among the acentric candidates the magnitude of the largest \(|d_{ij}|\) is controlled by (i) anion polarizability (Te > Se > P > S > Cl/O), (ii) constructive alignment of the NLO-active units (polar space groups such as \(P6_3mc\), \(P3m1\), \(Pna2_1\) outperform non-polar acentric groups such as \(I\overline{4}2d\) or \(F\overline{4}3m\)), and (iii) Miller’s rule (\(d\sim E_g^{-3}\)). Consequently a Pareto front in the \((|d_{ij}|,E_g)\) plane is obtained by selecting chemically and structurally distinct prototypes that successively trade gap for nonlinearity:

- zincblende BeSe (largest gap, modest \(d_{14}\));
- polar LiAlS\(_2\)-type (still wide-gap, larger \(d_{33}\) from aligned tetrahedra);
- defect-chalcopyrite ZnGa\(_2\)S\(_4\) (intermediate gap and \(d_{36}\));
- chalcopyrite CdSiP\(_2\) (exceptionally large \(d_{36}\) at \(E_g\simeq2.2\,\mathrm{eV}\));
- polar YCuTe\(_2\) (smallest gap, highest polarizability and \(d_{33}\)).

These five points are mutually non-dominated and span the widest hypervolume.

Final: c037, c057, c050, c030, c066
